{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work-list", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"facets": {}, "total-results": 393083, "items": [{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 12]], "date-time": "2026-08-12T12:36:36Z", "timestamp": 1786538196462, "version": "build-2736575974"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2012]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Professional athletes rely on sports agents to represent them in the business aspects of their profession.  Agents play a critical role in the athletes\u2019 lives by managing business affairs off the field, so that the athletes can focus on their performance on the field.  Most professional athletes rely on agents as trusted advisors for all aspects of their careers: to negotiate contracts, engage in marketing activities, develop athletes\u2019 brands, secure their financial futures, and prepare them for life after their playing careers. In such a key role in the athlete\u2019s life, great trust is put in the agent.  Violation of that trust by the agent that results in eliminating of an athlete\u2019s athletic eligibility, ruining an athlete\u2019s financial future, harming a collegiate athletic program, and interfering with professional contracts, is behavior that should be addressed by Congress.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.2138036", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2012, 9, 5]], "date-time": "2012-09-05T11:00:34Z", "timestamp": 1346842834000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 1, "title": ["Enough is Enough: The Case for Federal Regulation of Sport Agents"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"given": "Lisa P.", "family": "Masteralexis", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "James", "family": "Masteralexis", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Kevin", "family": "Snyder", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 12]], "date-time": "2026-08-12T11:59:59Z", "timestamp": 1786535999000}, "score": 20.61, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=2138036"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2012]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.2138036", "published": {"date-parts": [[2012]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T16:03:37Z", "timestamp": 1781280217915, "version": "3.54.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 10]]}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 10]], "date-time": "2025-06-10T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1749513600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Recent publications on AI Agents are anchoring on OAuth, specifically\nOAuth 2.1, for delegated access. The paper identifies key limitations of\nOAuth in this context, including coarse permission scopes, lack of\ndynamic policy enforcement, and insufficient support for multi-hop\ndelegation of authority. It then proposes complementary mechanisms such\nas policy-as-code enforcement, enhanced token delegation flows, and\nsecure AI agent architecture patterns to address these gaps. This\napproach aligns with emerging industry standards and research,\nmaintaining compatibility with existing OAuth infrastructure, while\nextending it to ensure AI agents act within human-intended bounds. The\nanalysis concludes that securing AI agents requires a layered\nauthorization stack that extends beyond OAuth alone.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.36227/techrxiv.174952577.74018032/v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 9]], "date-time": "2025-06-09T23:22:56Z", "timestamp": 1749511376000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["OAuth Is Not Enough Authorization Challenges for Autonomous AI Agents"], "prefix": "10.36227", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6077-5029", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Vivek", "family": "Chopra", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "263", "link": [{"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.36227/techrxiv.174952577.74018032/v1", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T15:25:18Z", "timestamp": 1781277918000}, "score": 19.631311, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/full/10.36227/techrxiv.174952577.74018032/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 10]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.174952577.74018032/v1", "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 10]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T15:01:47Z", "timestamp": 1785337307778, "version": "3.55.0"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/copyright-policy/copyright-basics"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Modern web interfaces are increasingly difficult to use with screen readers, particularly when pages update dynamically or hide important structure behind visual layout. Recent UI agents can act on such interfaces; however, for assistive agents to be truly useful, they must behave as collaborators that keep users informed and in control, rather than as tools that simply take actions on users' behalf. Most existing benchmarks judge systems primarily by task completion, without assessing how well they explain their actions or support user oversight. We introduce NeXUI, a benchmark for assistive agents that must navigate interfaces while explaining each step in clear language for nonvisual use. NeXUI pairs realistic user goals with instrumented interface states, enabling agents to reason from both visual context and structural information. Its evaluation measures safety, efficiency, and task success, while also checking whether explanations are grounded in the interface state. In our experiments, we find that NeXUI remains challenging even for stateof-the-art foundation models, with Gemini-3.5-Flash achieving only a 44% success rate and poor explanation scores, making it a useful foundation for future research and development. By focusing on navigation, explanation, and user control, NeXUI provides a clearer way to study agents that can support blind and visually impaired users in modern computing environments.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7113240", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T14:11:14Z", "timestamp": 1785334274000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Navigation Alone Is Not Enough: Evaluating Explanatory Assistive UI Agents"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4780-2624", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Santosh", "family": "Patapati", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 7, 29]], "date-time": "2026-07-29T14:11:14Z", "timestamp": 1785334274000}, "score": 19.27964, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=7113240"}}, "issued": {"date-pa